# ---- hub (one machine, e.g. your Mac) ----
HUB_PORT=8787
# Long random string; every device, the phone page and the glasses app need it.
HUB_TOKEN=change-me
# The voice router and all agents run on your Claude Code login (run `claude auth login` once
# on each machine). No API key needed. Optionally pick the router's model (default: your Claude Code default):
# ROUTER_MODEL=opus
# Logins can expire; for an always-on hub, create a long-lived token once with `claude setup-token`
# and put it here (keeps working for about a year, still your subscription, no API key):
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# Studio (vibe-coding mini-apps): the model used when none is picked (phone picker or "…with the fast
# model"): fast (Claude Sonnet 5.5 at low effort; measured faster than Haiku here), balanced (Claude Sonnet 5.5, default), best (Claude Opus 5.5),
# deepseek, codex or an OpenRouter model (e.g. openrouter:z-ai/glm-5.3). An exact Claude model id also works (it replaces that tier's model).
# STUDIO_MODEL=balanced
# DeepSeek in Studio (optional; the picker offers it only when this is set). Runs the same Claude Code
# agent against DeepSeek's Anthropic-compatible API, with this key for those builds only. Create a key
# at platform.deepseek.com > API keys (prepaid balance needed). Model default: deepseek-flash (newest).
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-v4-pro
# OpenRouter in Studio (optional; the picker shows an "OpenRouter" group only when this is set). Runs the
# same Claude Code agent against OpenRouter's Anthropic-compatible API (https://openrouter.ai/api), with
# this key for those builds only. Key at openrouter.ai/settings/keys (credits needed). Default models:
# DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, GLM 5.3, Kimi K3, Qwen 3.8 Flash, Gemini 3.8 Flash, GPT-6 Luna. Say "make it with
# kimi" (or "openrouter" for the first). OPENROUTER_MODELS replaces the list: slug=Label, comma-separated.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_MODELS=z-ai/glm-5.3=GLM,moonshotai/kimi-k3=Kimi,qwen/qwen3.8-flash
# Codex in Studio uses the Codex CLI's own login when it's installed (see CODEX_BIN below);
# optionally pick its model: STUDIO_CODEX_MODEL=
# Live translation: optionally a faster model (default: your Claude Code default)
# TRANSLATE_MODEL=haiku
# Chinese in Translate shows hanzi + pinyin + meaning. Pinyin tone style: marks (nǐ hǎo) or numbers
# (ni3 hao3, plain ASCII) if the glasses font has no tone-marked vowels. TRANSLATE_GLASSES_HANZI=0
# leaves characters off the glasses (pinyin + meaning only) if it has no CJK glyphs; the phone always
# shows them. Both are defaults: the phone page can change them per session.
# TRANSLATE_PINYIN_TONES=marks
# TRANSLATE_GLASSES_HANZI=1

# Speech-to-text for glasses audio (the Even SDK has none). Without a key it runs on this Mac (Apple
# on-device speech, then whisper.cpp). Soniox (cloud, much better live, zh/en mixing; key at console.soniox.com):
SONIOX_API_KEY=
# Live engine, default auto (soniox if its key is set, else apple, else whisper): STT_ENGINE=apple
# A list sets the auto order (fallbacks go down it): STT_ENGINE=wispr,soniox,apple,whisper
# Wispr Flow (optional, cloud; its dictation cleanup: auto-edits, no "um"s, names right). The API is by
# approval only: ask enterprise@wisprflow.ai, then platform.wisprflow.ai > API Keys. With the key set,
# each push-to-talk clip's final text comes from Wispr (the live words are still streamed while you
# hold); WISPR_PTT=0 turns that off. Live captions stay on Soniox/Apple unless STT_ENGINE lists wispr.
WISPR_API_KEY=
# WISPR_TERMS=Tanay,Even G2
# Or push-to-talk finals from an OpenRouter transcription model (uses OPENROUTER_API_KEY), e.g.
# deepgram/nova-3 (default for plain "openrouter"), elevenlabs/scribe-v2, assemblyai/universal-3-6-pro:
# STT_BATCH_ENGINE=openrouter:deepgram/nova-3
# (STT_BATCH_ENGINE=wispr | openrouter:<model> | none; unset = Wispr when WISPR_API_KEY is set)
# Batch fallback (default: the push-to-talk engine above, then soniox if its key is set). Pick one:
# STT_PROVIDER=deepgram
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=
# STT_PROVIDER=whisper-cpp
# WHISPER_BIN=whisper-cli
# WHISPER_MODEL=/path/to/ggml-base.en.bin

# Navigate map: Google roads (Maps Static API; optional, else OpenStreetMap lines). Get a key in the
# Google Cloud console (console.cloud.google.com > APIs & Services): enable "Maps Static API", create an
# API key and restrict it to Maps Static API, Places API (New) and Routes API (the same key is used for all three). The hub calls Google itself, so don't restrict it by website.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Your Google account (Calendar on Today + voice, Gmail by voice). An OAuth "Desktop app" client from
# your own Google Cloud project; steps also in docs/PLAYBOOK.md ("Google account"):
#  1. console.cloud.google.com > create (or pick) a project.
#  2. APIs & Services > Library: enable "Google Calendar API" and "Gmail API".
#  3. APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen (Google Auth Platform): User type External, app name e.g.
#     "Jarvis", your address as support + developer contact. Leave Publishing status on Testing.
#  4. Audience > Test users > Add users: your own Gmail address.
#  5. Data access > Add or remove scopes (optional, the app asks anyway): calendar.readonly,
#     calendar.events, gmail.readonly, gmail.compose, gmail.send.
#  6. Clients (Credentials) > Create client > OAuth client ID > Application type "Desktop app" > Create.
#     Copy the client ID and secret here.
#  7. On the Mac: npm run google:connect (or Connect on the phone page) and allow everything. The refresh
#     token goes to ~/.g2-orchestrator/google-token.json (mode 600). In Testing, Google expires it after
#     7 days: run google:connect again then.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Seconds a send-email / add-event card waits for your tap before it counts as no (default 60).
# GOOGLE_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S=60

# Seconds before an unanswered approval is denied (SDK agents) / handed back to the terminal (hooks).
APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S=600
HOOK_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S=120

# ---- device daemon (every machine that runs agents) ----
# Use the hub's Tailscale address from other machines, e.g. http://100.x.y.z:8787
G2_HUB_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787
G2_HUB_TOKEN=change-me
# G2_NODE_NAME=macbook
# G2_DEFAULT_CWD=~/projects
# Project picker: JSON array of {"name":"Project name","path":"/absolute/folder"}.
# The default is ~/.g2-orchestrator/projects.json. Paths stay on the computer running this daemon.
# G2_PROJECTS_FILE=~/.g2-orchestrator/projects.json
# Codex uses its own CLI login (`codex login`); it does not need an OpenAI API key here.
# CODEX_BIN=/absolute/path/to/codex
# Tools agents may use without asking:
# G2_BYPASS_PERMISSIONS=1   # skip all approvals
# G2_AUTO_ALLOW=Read,Glob,Grep,LS,TodoWrite,WebSearch
# Another computer: scripts/join-worker.sh pairs it with a one-time code and writes G2_HUB_URL and its
# own G2_HUB_TOKEN (g2w_..., revocable from the phone page) here; the hub's HUB_TOKEN never goes there.
# Computer use (macOS; off unless set): agents may drive this computer's screen, every step approved on
# the glasses. See docs/PLAYBOOK.md "Home computer".
# G2_COMPUTER_USE=1
# G2_COMPUTER_APPROVAL=step      # step = ask before every action batch (default), task = once per task
# G2_COMPUTER_THUMBNAIL=1        # the latest screen as a small tile in Agents mode (off by default)
# G2_COMPUTER_DENY_APPS=Banking App,Other   # more apps that always need a fresh tap
# G2_COMPUTER_KILL_FILE=~/.g2-orchestrator/computer-stop   # while this file exists, nothing runs
# G2_COMPUTER_MODEL=           # default: your Claude Code default
